> Cost and interpretation

For dimension d, N live points, I replacements and W walk proposals, producer
work is O(Nd+I(N+Wd)), plus actual likelihood costs; linear worst-point scans
are included. Live/result storage is O((N+I)d). An owned proposal trace
additionally scales with IWd, so the quota measures logical history rather than
peak process memory. Callback cost and poor finite-walk mixing can dominate.

Information is recomputed from every normalized dead and final-live weight as
Σw(log L−log Z), then clipped at zero for the returned heuristic sqrt(H/N). This
corrects the old omission of final points and the first discarded contribution.
The approximation and finite walk can bias both evidence and the diagnostic. We
retain the original Gaussian comparison while removing the old assertion that
one estimate lying within a displayed uncertainty validates that uncertainty.

[Skilling (2006), Nested sampling for general Bayesian computation|https://doi.org/10.1214/06-BA127]
describes the method and its ideal sampling assumptions. These assumptions
explain why the recorded mass identity is a precise accounting check while
Gaussian accuracy and finite-walk performance require empirical assessment.

